<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="nevadensin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Nevadensin is a lipophilic methylated flavonoid found in sweet basil (|FRAME: TAX-39350|). Flavonoids are found throughout the plant kingdom where they play diverse and important physiological roles |CITS: [21880658]|. Many of these structurally diverse compounds also have an impact on human health and the enzymes involved in their production are attractive candidates for metabolic engineering |CITS: [21732240]|. To date, the end product of this pathway, |FRAME: CPD-15468|, has only been reported in a small number of |FRAME: TAX-91827| species, such as in the |FRAME: TAX-255818| and |FRAME: TAX-39173| genuses |CITS: [Suksamrarn03][11281133]|, but, it may be found in additional plant species in the future. Regiospecific O-methylations as well as the hydroxylations at positions 6 and 8 of the A ring of |FRAME: CPD-15468| are distinctive features of this compound and several related compounds that may contribute to their lipophilicity. Given the phylogenetic dispersion of these compounds efforts are underway to determine if they are synthesized in the same manner in diverse species |CITS: [23184958]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Flavone metabolism in basil appears to occur in complex manner. |FRAME: CPD-15468 Nevadensin| is formed via |FRAME: CPD-15477|, which is formed from |FRAME: CPD-431| in multiple routes, as described in |FRAME: PWY-7325|. |FRAME: CPD-15477 Salvigenin| is 8-hydroxylated by a Rieske-type oxygenase (|FRAME: G-124265|) to |FRAME: CPD-15476| |CITS: [25139498]|. Next |FRAME: CPD-15476| is converted to |FRAME: CPD-15468| by &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methylation of position 8 of the A ring and O-demethylation at the 7 position of the same ring.The 7-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-demethylase involved in the production of |FRAME: CPD-15468| is a 2-oxoglutarate-dependent dioxygenase |CITS: [25378691]|. While a candidate for the gene encoding the methyltranferase has been identified, its activity was not verified experimentally because its substrates were not available at the time |CITS: [23747095]|.</Description>
